// crew#516 CP5, founder's locked spec (crew#66 5451926212, Phase 2), verbatim shape: "access is
// structurally locked down by identity, allowing only the Kubernetes cluster tag (tag:k8s) to
// communicate with the Mac host (tag:founder-mac)". ${FOUNDER_MAC_USER} is the one placeholder
// this file carries (LAW 46: no literal username in git); bin/idp-tailscale-policy substitutes it
// from clusters/oke/estate-config.yaml before PUTting the ACL, the same substitution source Flux
// uses for the Kubernetes manifests. Applied by CI (bin/idp-tailscale-policy apply, oke-check's
// apply job), never by hand in the admin console — the no-toil rule this file exists to satisfy.
//
// crew#562 refines that spec, and the refinement is why the second rule below exists. The founder
// then asked for iPhone -> Mac remote desk over this same tailnet (Moonlight to Sunshine, plus an
// iOS Shortcut that SSHes `caffeinate -u -t 2`), and his acceptance line is "i see it seanlessly
// nno firction". Applying THIS file as it stood would have made that impossible, silently, and
// only after it had already been seen working:
//
//   - A policy file with an `acls` section is deny-by-default; the tailnet's default "allow all"
//     rule is replaced, not extended (tailscale.com/kb/1018/acls: "Using a default deny policy
//     prevents communication between devices without explicit access to each other").
//   - "Applying a tag to a device removes any user-based authentication ... A device cannot
//     simultaneously use tag-based and user-based identities" (tailscale.com/kb/1068/tags). The
//     moment the Mac runs `--advertise-tags=tag:founder-mac` it stops being the founder's device
//     and becomes a tagged one, so his phone reaches it only through a rule that names it.
//
// So with one rule (tag:k8s -> tag:founder-mac:22) his own iPhone matches nothing: Moonlight gets
// no route to Sunshine and the Shortcut's SSH is refused. Nothing would have gone red — the ACL
// PUT succeeds, the operator is happy, and the only symptom is the founder's stream not starting.
// It has not bitten yet purely because the OAuth client this applier reads does not exist, so the
// policy has never actually been PUT; the day someone creates it, the remote desk breaks.
//
// The lock-down the founder asked for is about which MACHINES may reach the Mac. His own devices
// are him. `autogroup:member` is every human member of this tailnet, and the ports are named
// rather than `*`: 22 for the Shortcut, and 47984-48010, the block Sunshine listens on (TCP
// 47984/47989/47990/48010 and UDP 47998/47999/48000/48002/48010, docs.lizardbyte.dev network
// configuration). The cluster still gets exactly port 22 and nothing else.
{
  "tagOwners": {
    "tag:k8s":          [],
    "tag:founder-mac":  [],
  },
  "acls": [
    {"action": "accept", "src": ["tag:k8s"], "dst": ["tag:founder-mac:22"]},
    // crew#562: the founder's own phone and laptop reaching his tagged Mac. Without this the
    // remote desk cannot work at all, because tagging took his user identity off the device.
    {"action": "accept", "src": ["autogroup:member"],
     "dst":    ["tag:founder-mac:22", "tag:founder-mac:47984-48010"]},
  ],
  "ssh": [
    {
      "action": "check",
      "src":    ["tag:k8s"],
      "dst":    ["tag:founder-mac"],
      "users":  ["${FOUNDER_MAC_USER}"],
    },
    // crew#562, second half of the same trap. The founder's sitting runs `tailscale up --ssh`, so
    // Tailscale intercepts port 22 on that device and the `ssh` block -- not the `acls` block --
    // decides the Shortcut's SSH. An acl rule alone would still leave it refused.
    {
      "action": "accept",
      "src":    ["autogroup:member"],
      "dst":    ["tag:founder-mac"],
      "users":  ["${FOUNDER_MAC_USER}"],
    },
  ],
}
